[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: CQRS in Database Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS) is an architectural pattern that separates read and write operations for a data store. By decoupling the models used for updating data (Commands) from the models used for reading data (Queries), systems can scale, perform, and secure each side independently."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of CQRS"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Commands: Operations that mutate state (Create, Update, Delete). They do not return data other than success or failure, and they enforce business logic and invariants."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Queries: Operations that read state. They do not mutate state and are typically optimized for fast retrieval, often bypassing domain logic."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Traditional Database Design vs. CQRS"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a traditional CRUD architecture, a single database schema and model serve both reads and writes. As applications grow, queries require complex joins and aggregations that conflict with the normalized structure needed for efficient writes."},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"With CQRS, you can use a highly normalized relational database for the Write side to guarantee ACID compliance, and a denormalized read-optimized store (such as Elasticsearch, MongoDB, or materialized views) for the Read side."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example: Write Model"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Write Side: Domain Model and Command Handler
public class CreateOrderCommand {
    public Guid OrderId { get; set; }
    public string CustomerId { get; set; }
    public decimal Amount { get; set; }
}

public class OrderCommandHandler {
    private readonly WriteDbContext _context;

    public void Handle(CreateOrderCommand command) {
        var order = new Order(command.OrderId, command.CustomerId, command.Amount);
        _context.Orders.Add(order);
        _context.SaveChanges();
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example: Read Model"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Read Side: DTO and Query Handler
public class OrderDetailsDto {
    public string OrderId { get; set; }
    public string CustomerName { get; set; }
    public decimal TotalAmount { get; set; }
}

public class GetOrderQueryHandler {
    string connectionString;

    public OrderDetailsDto Handle(string orderId) {
        using var connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString);
        return connection.QueryFirstOrDefault<OrderDetailsDto>(
            "SELECT OrderId, CustomerName, TotalAmount FROM OrderReadModel WHERE OrderId = @Id", 
            new { Id = orderId });
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Synchronization Strategies"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When the read and write databases are physically separated, synchronization becomes a crucial challenge. Common patterns include Event Sourcing (publishing domain events on every write) and Database Change Data Capture (CDC) tools like Debezium or Kafka Connect to stream changes from the write store to the read store asynchronously."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use CQRS"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High-performance systems where read and write workloads scale independently."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Complex domain models where business logic is heavily entangled with reporting queries."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Collaborative domains where multiple users act on the same data concurrently."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"CQRS introduces architectural complexity, particularly regarding eventual consistency and data synchronization. However, for high-scale enterprise database design, it provides unmatched flexibility to optimize reads and writes separately."}]
